---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, isCommodityNode, site } from '../lib/site-variant';

if (!isCommodityNode) return Astro.redirect('/blog/');
const canonicalUrl = absoluteUrl('/contact/');
---

<Base
  title="Contact"
  metaTitle="Contact CommodityNode"
  description="Public channels for CommodityNode corrections, source questions, security reports, and product feedback."
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
>
  <article class="static-page prose">
    <p class="section-label">Contact</p>
    <h1>Send the issue to the channel that can verify it.</h1>
    <p class="article-dek">
      CommodityNode keeps editorial and source discussions public whenever they contain no
      confidential information. That makes the response and resulting change auditable.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial correction or source question</h2>
    <p>
      Use the <a href={`${site.sourceUrl}/issues/new`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">public issue tracker</a>.
      Include the exact page, claim, source URL, and retrieval date.
    </p>

    <h2>Security report</h2>
    <p>
      Do not publish exploit details or secrets. Use the repository's
      <a href={`${site.sourceUrl}/security/advisories/new`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">private security advisory</a>
      channel. Never include an API key, access token, or personal data in a public issue.
    </p>

    <h2>Product and accessibility feedback</h2>
    <p>
      Open a public issue with the device, browser, page URL, expected behavior, and what
      happened. Accessibility reports are triaged as product defects, not feature requests.
    </p>

    <h2>Response expectations</h2>
    <p>
      The project does not promise 24/7 monitoring. Critical security and false-publication
      reports take priority; other reports are handled in the public queue as capacity allows.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
